## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a practical on-device data structure with incremental updates, offline-first UX, and a clear 12‑month cost plan, including privacy considerations.

## Key Concepts

- Local inverted index and incremental updates
- Offline-first data paths and cache invalidation
- Delta-based cloud sync and conflict resolution
- Resource budgeting: storage, CPU, memory, latency, energy
- Privacy, compliance, and user consent handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal incremental inverted index (conceptual)
class InvertedIndex {
  constructor() {
    this.index = {}; // term -> Set of note IDs
  }
  addNote(id, text) {
    text.split(/\W+/).forEach(t => {
      if (!t) return;
      const w = t.toLowerCase();
      (this.index[w] ||= new Set()).add(id);
    });
  }
  search(q) {
    return q
      .toLowerCase()
      .split(/\s+/)
      .flatMap(w => Array.from(this.index[w] || []));
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you estimate index maintenance energy and storage growth over time?
- How would you handle search latency guarantees on mid-range devices with thousands of notes?
- What privacy safeguards would you implement for on-device indexing and sync deltas?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of edge compute architecture, privacy-preserving data transfer, and cross-region cost modeling. It forces candidates to make trade-offs between latency, bandwidth, and regulatory compliance while demonstrating practical planning skills.

## Key Concepts

- **Edge locality and regional caching**: Evaluating feature flags at the CDN edge minimizes round-trip time to origin servers
- **Privacy-preserving deltas**: Shipping only anonymized changes rather than full state reduces bandwidth and exposure risk
- **Data residency and encryption**: Compliance with GDPR, CCPA, and regional data sovereignty requirements adds infrastructure cost
- **Phased rollout**: Gradual deployment with canary releases reduces risk across regions
- **Drift detection and rollback**: Monitoring for configuration drift and automated rollback procedures ensure reliability
- **Break-even analysis**: Quantifying backend load reduction, latency gains, and operational savings against implementation costs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal CBA data model sketch
class RegionCost {
  constructor(region, cacheKB, latencyMs, deltaKB) {
    this.region = region;
    this.cacheKB = cacheKB;
    this.latencyMs = latencyMs;
    this.deltaKB = deltaKB;
  }

  monthlyBandwidth(deltaKB, requestsPerMonth) {
    return (deltaKB * requestsPerMonth) / 1024; // MB/month
  }

  annualCost(monthlyBandwidthMB, complianceTier) {
    const bandwidthCost = monthlyBandwidthMB * 12 * 0.05; // $0.05/MB
    const complianceCost = complianceTier * 5000; // $5k-$15k/region
    return bandwidthCost + complianceCost;
  }
}

const regions = [
  new RegionCost('us-east', 32, 1.2, 0.5),
  new RegionCost('eu-west', 24, 1.8, 0.4),
  new RegionCost('ap-south', 16, 2.5, 0.3)
];
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle on CBA: gating cross‑app features via an on‑device policy engine, balancing UX, energy, and safety with cloud updates.

## Key Concepts
- On‑device policy engines and gating rules
- Resource awareness (battery, thermal, memory)
- Telemetry footprint and privacy compliance
- Rollout strategy and break‑even analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
function shouldRunFeature(state, policy) {
  if (state.battery < 20) return false;
  if (state.temperature > 45) return false;
  if (state.freeMemory < 200) return false;
  return policy.allowsFeature;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify risk of safety incidents vs false positives?
- What metrics would drive policy update cadence and rollouts across device generations?
